    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/louis-botha.jpg" alt="Louis Botha" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Louisbotha_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was created in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> (1899–1902), in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> had conquered the South African Republic (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transvaal">Transvaal</a>) and the Orange Free State. By the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-vereeniging">Treaty of Vereeniging</a> in 1902 the two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boers">Boer</a> republics became British colonies, and in 1906–1907 they received responsible government, which brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> generals <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Louis Botha</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a> to power in the Transvaal. Rivalry among the four colonies over railways and customs duties, together with a wish among white politicians to settle ‘native policy’ on a common footing, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> of 1908–1909, which met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durban">Durban</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-town">Cape Town</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a>. Its draft constitution was passed by the British Parliament as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa-act-1909">South Africa Act 1909</a>, and the Union came into being on 31 May 1910, eight years to the day after Vereeniging. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colony-of-natal">Natal Colony</a>, the Transvaal Colony and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-river-colony">Orange River Colony</a> became its four provinces. As a compromise, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a> became the administrative capital, Cape Town the legislative capital and Bloemfontein the seat of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appellate-division-of-the-supreme-court-of-south-africa">Appellate Division</a>. Botha, who formally founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a> in 1911, became the first prime minister, and the Union joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> as a self-governing dominion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje.jpg" alt="Sol Plaatje" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje">Sol Plaatje</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sol_Plaatje_002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AnonymousUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union’s economy rested on mining, above all the gold of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">Witwatersrand</a>, which made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> the world’s largest gold producer, together with the diamonds of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kimberley">Kimberley</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and commercial farming. The mines depended on large numbers of African migrant workers, recruited from the reserves and from neighbouring territories such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, who lived in compounds on low wages, while skilled work was reserved for whites under the Mines and Works Act of 1911. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natives-land-act-1913">Natives Land Act</a> of 1913 confined African land ownership to reserves covering about 7 per cent of the country, raised to about 13 per cent by the Native Trust and Land Act of 1936; the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sol-plaatje">Sol Plaatje</a> condemned it in <em>Native Life in South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a></em> (1916). Urban segregation followed with the Natives (Urban Areas) Act of 1923. Among whites, rapid urbanisation created a large class of impoverished <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a>, the ‘poor white problem’, which governments addressed through protected employment on the railways and in state industries such as the Iron and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">Steel</a> Corporation (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iscor">Iscor</a>), founded in 1928. Manufacturing expanded strongly during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. Society was divided into legally defined groups of Europeans, Africans, Coloured people and Indians, the last including the community among whom <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mohandas Gandhi</a> worked until 1914.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delville-wood"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-delville-wood.jpg" alt="Battle of Delville Wood" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delville-wood">Battle of Delville Wood</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Battle_of_the_Somme,_1916.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SVG version, and changes: Grandiose; Original map:Worldwari map13 largerview.jpg, US Military; Inset map: Giro720, File:France blank1.svg, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-defence-force-south-africa">Union Defence Force</a> was established by the Defence Act of 1912. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began in 1914, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Botha</a>’s decision to invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-south-west-africa">German South West Africa</a> provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maritz-rebellion">Maritz Rebellion</a>, a rising of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> commandos under leaders such as Christiaan de Wet and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manie-maritz">Manie Maritz</a>, which government forces suppressed by early 1915. Botha then led the campaign that forced the German surrender in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> in July 1915, and South African troops fought in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Smuts</a> and on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-front-world-war-i">Western Front</a>, where the South African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigade">Brigade</a> suffered heavy losses at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-delville-wood">Battle of Delville Wood</a> in July 1916. The army was also used at home, most violently during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">the Rand</a> Revolt of 1922, when Smuts sent troops, artillery and aircraft to crush an armed strike by white mineworkers. In September 1939 parliament voted by 80 to 67 to enter the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and Smuts replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a> as prime minister. Volunteer forces fought in the East African campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, where some 10,000 South Africans were captured at the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tobruk">Tobruk</a> in June 1942, then at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-battle-of-el-alamein">Second Battle of El Alamein</a> and in Italy. Black South Africans were officially confined to non-combatant roles.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration-of-1926"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration-of-1926.jpg" alt="Balfour Declaration of 1926" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration-of-1926">Balfour Declaration of 1926</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ImperialConference.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union was a parliamentary state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-system">Westminster model</a>, with a governor-general representing the British Crown, a Senate, a House of Assembly and four provinces with elected councils. The franchise was the decisive issue. The Cape kept its qualified non-racial franchise, under which some African and Coloured men could vote, but only whites could sit in parliament, and in the other provinces the vote was reserved for whites; white women were enfranchised in 1930. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-botha">Botha</a> (1910–1919) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Smuts</a> (1919–1924 and 1939–1948) led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-african-party">South African Party</a> and sought reconciliation between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaners</a> and English-speakers within the empire. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-b-m-hertzog">J. B. M. Hertzog</a>, who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> in 1914, governed from 1924 in a pact with the Labour Party, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaans">Afrikaans</a> an official language in 1925 and won recognition of dominion equality in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balfour-declaration-of-1926">Balfour Declaration of 1926</a>, confirmed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/statute-of-westminster-1931">Statute of Westminster</a> in 1931 and the Status of the Union Act of 1934. Hertzog and Smuts merged their parties into the United Party in 1934, and in 1936 Cape Africans were removed from the common voters’ roll. In 1948 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/d-f-malan">D. F. Malan</a>’s Reunited National Party won power and began apartheid with laws such as the Population Registration Act and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> of 1950.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/kliptown"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kliptown.jpg" alt="Kliptown" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/kliptown">Kliptown</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kliptown_is_one_of_the_most_destitute_areas_of_the_large_Soweto_township,_South_Africa,_November_2012._(S._Honorine_VOA).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">S. Honorine VOA, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union shaped twentieth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and much of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Its laws on land, labour and residence created the legal framework that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> extended into apartheid after 1948, and the division of land under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/natives-land-act-1913">Natives Land Act</a> of 1913 became the basis of the later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustans</a>. Organised resistance also began under the Union: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">South African Native National Congress</a>, renamed the African National Congress in 1923, was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bloemfontein">Bloemfontein</a> in 1912; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clements-kadalie">Clements Kadalie</a>’s Industrial and Commercial Workers’ Union organised tens of thousands of workers in the 1920s; and in the 1950s the ANC and its allies ran the Defiance Campaign of 1952 and adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freedom-charter">Freedom Charter</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kliptown">Kliptown</a> in 1955. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Gandhi</a> developed his method of <em>satyagraha</em>, non-violent resistance, among South African Indians before returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Internationally, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Smuts</a> helped shape the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> and is credited with drafting the preamble to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-charter">United Nations Charter</a> in 1945, yet the Union’s refusal to place <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/namibia">South West Africa</a> under UN trusteeship and its racial laws made it a target of growing criticism at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a>. As a leading dominion it also took part in the evolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> into the Commonwealth.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom.jpg" alt="J. G. Strijdom" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom">J. G. Strijdom</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hans_Strijdom_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Union did not collapse; it was transformed into a republic by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afrikaners">Afrikaner</a> nationalists who had governed it since 1948. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom">J. G. Strijdom</a> (1954–1958) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a> (from 1958) the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> deepened apartheid, removed Coloured voters from the common roll in 1956 after a long constitutional struggle, and planned separate ethnic ‘<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">homelands</a>’ under the Promotion of Bantu Self-Government Act of 1959. On 21 March 1960 police at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharpeville-massacre">Sharpeville</a> fired on a crowd protesting against the pass laws, killing 69 people; the government declared a state of emergency and banned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">ANC</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a>. Verwoerd then held a referendum of white voters on 5 October 1960, in which about 52 per cent voted for a republic. At the Commonwealth Prime Ministers’ Conference in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> in March 1961, faced with criticism of apartheid from other members, Verwoerd withdrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>’s application to remain in the Commonwealth. On 31 May 1961 the Union of South <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> became the Republic of South Africa, with a state president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/c-r-swart">C. R. Swart</a>, in place of the governor-general and the British monarch. Its territory, institutions and racial laws passed unchanged to the republic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Union of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> joined four British colonies into a self-governing dominion that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s strongest industrial economy and an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in both world wars, while its land, labour and franchise laws built the legal structure of segregation that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a> turned into apartheid after 1948.</em></p>

